Seven exterior vertices for thirteen hexagons per edge
======================================================

Let G be a finite simple quartic graph of girth six in which every edge lies on exactly thirteen hexagons. Fix an edge uv. Its two depth-two rooted ternary trees have 26 distinct vertices. Their two nine-element leaf sets L,R can be joined only by edges from L to R: any additional same-side or interior edge would close a cycle shorter than six. Every such cross edge determines one hexagon through uv, so there are thirteen cross edges. The total missing leaf incidences on each side is D=27-13=14.

Write Y for the vertices outside these two trees, t=|Y|. All 28 missing leaf incidences have their other endpoint in Y, and no other old vertex has residual degree. Summing degrees in Y gives 4t=28+2e(Y), whence t>=7 and n>=33. Counting hexagon-edge incidences gives the integer number of hexagons 13n/3; thus 3 divides n. To prove n>=36 it therefore suffices to exclude n=33.

At n=33, Y has seven vertices and e(Y)=0. Every vertex of Y has four neighbours among the old leaves, at most one in each of the three branches on either side, since two in one branch would close a quadrangle. Consequently its side degrees are in {1,2,3} and add to four. On each side the seven positive degrees sum to fourteen. If a_i counts degree i on a side, a_1=a_3=a and a_2=7-2a, so 0<=a<=3. The two sides have the same a because degrees are complementary. The total number of unordered same-side pairs sharing a neighbour in Y is therefore a_2+3a_3=7+a, the same on both sides and in [7,10].

The acquired root-pair identity says that, for two of the three branches L_i,L_j and the remaining branch L_k, sum_w |N(w) cap L_i||N(w) cap L_j| equals the number of cross edges incident with L_k. This counts hexagons through the pair of root edges uL_i,uL_j, whose count is thirteen minus the count through the third root edge; its exact derivation is retained from the independently accepted lambda15 manuscript. Summing over the three pairs, the total prescribed number is thirteen. A leaf on the opposite side with missing degree d has 3-d old cross neighbours, in distinct branches, and contributes binom(3-d,2) to the old total. Hence the required exterior contribution on each side is

  f = 13 - sum binom(3-d,2) = (42-sum d^2)/2.

Thus f_L=f_R in [7,10] is a necessary condition. We retain it exactly. The earlier partition-capacity bound is omitted altogether in the lambda13 traversal, so no new capacity calculation or premise is required.

Complete boundary coverage proceeds as follows. Partition the fourteen distinguished missing left incidences into exactly seven nonempty blocks, each containing distinct leaves in different branches; prohibit common old neighbours and repeated leaf pairs, and impose the three exact left root-pair sums. First-appearance block naming covers every unlabelled partition. Each block becomes one exterior vertex. Its remaining capacity is four minus its size, in {1,2,3}. Assign the fourteen right incidences to those seven capacities. Repeated copies at a single right leaf use strictly increasing exterior indices, choosing the unique order for the same neighbour set. Reject a proposed edge only if its endpoints have current distance below five, or if an exact right root-pair total would be exceeded. Those quantities can only increase. Require all capacities and right pair totals at completion. There are no further vertices or edges. Testing the completed graph therefore exhausts all possible order33 completions.

This is the same coupled boundary mechanism as the accepted lambda15 component, with seven rather than six exterior vertices, fourteen rather than twelve incidences per side, target13 rather than15, and order33 rather than32. These are fresh target obligations, not evidence supplied by the earlier census. l044 independently checked the displayed seven-vertex and divisibility reductions as a preliminary read, without contributing a target enumeration or final acceptance.

The left partition search also uses the exact size distribution before completion. With a=f-7, an existing part of size three remains size three, and a part of size at least two cannot become a singleton. Thus at most a current parts may have size three and at most 7-a may have size at least two. If the remaining incidences cannot create enough new parts to reach seven, the branch is also impossible. These monotone bounds merely discard partial partitions that cannot have the required final size distribution.
